{
  "abstract": "The moving sofa problem (Moser 1966) asks for the largest area of a connected planar shape that can be moved around the right-angled corner of a hallway of unit width. Gerver (1992) constructed a sofa of area 2.21953..., which Romik (2018) described by an explicit system of equations, and conjectured that it is optimal. Jineon Baek (arXiv:2411.19826, 2024) proved that Gerver's sofa has the maximum area. This repository formalizes Baek's entire proof in Lean 4 with Mathlib. The Challenge states, in Mathlib's vocabulary only: Romik's system of equations has exactly one solution with phi in [0.039, 0.04] and theta in [0.68, 0.69], so Gerver's sofa is well defined; Gerver's sofa has area between 2.2192 and 2.2199; and Gerver's sofa is a moving sofa whose area is at least the area of every moving sofa. Every numbered result of the paper is proved. This includes Theorem 8.4.1, on the structure of Gerver's sofa, which the paper states without proof and which is proved here from Romik's equations by rigorous interval arithmetic. It also includes the results the paper takes from the literature: Schneider's area formula for planar convex bodies, and the existence and uniqueness of the solution of Romik's system, which Romik asserts without proof. Only Lean's three standard axioms are used. An accompanying audit (REPORT.md) checks the paper against its LaTeX source. Two statements of the paper are false as written: Definition 3.2.5, which makes Proposition 3.3.5 and Lemma 3.4.2 false, and one direction of Proposition 5.1.4. The proof of Theorem 6.1.2 is invalid as written. The audit also records smaller gaps and slips. Every result survives in its intended form, and the main theorem holds. The repository also proves, and the Challenge states, that Gerver's sofa is the only moving sofa of maximum area up to rigid motions: every moving sofa with the area of Gerver's sofa is mapped onto it, as a set, by a rotation followed by a translation. Baek's paper does not prove this, and Google DeepMind's formal-conjectures lists it as an open problem; we know of no earlier proof but have not searched the literature systematically. The argument was written by ChatGPT Pro 6 (OpenAI) for this repository (docs/uniqueness/20-complete-paper-proof.md) and has not been peer reviewed. Its formal proof uses the definitions and results of the formalization of Baek's paper: it selects polygon approximations of a given maximizing cap, shows that every maximizing right-angle cap satisfies Baek's injectivity condition, extends the motion of a maximizing sofa to a right angle, and identifies the cap with Gerver's through the equality case of Baek's upper bound and the regular closedness of Gerver's sofa.",
